  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in 2017 by Peter Lang in the established \"Travail et Soci\u00e9t\u00e9 / Work and Society\" series, this book examines austerity policy implementation under the Europe 2020 strategy in Spain. It has accumulated 3 citations to date, with uptake concentrated in education policy and social inequality research. The work is open access and held in at least 4 library collections, indicating modest but sustained institutional reach aligned with policy-relevant scholarship on European social conditions.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Javier Ramos and Esther del Campo's examination of austerity and the Europe 2020 Strategy implementation occupies a focused niche in European political economy and social policy studies. The book's primary concepts\u2014austerity and political science\u2014reflect its grounding in institutional and policy analysis, with secondary relevance to geography and law. Published within a reputable multilingual series dedicated to labor, work, and social policy questions, the work benefits from an established editorial framework and peer infrastructure that emphasize European labor relations and employment policy.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation record, though modest at 3 citing works, demonstrates meaningful intellectual traction within specific research communities. Citations span 2017 to 2020 and appear across diverse publication types\u2014a peer-reviewed journal article on social innovation in the European Union, a chapter on education and inequality across Finland, Spain, and Brazil, and an independently published atlas on social innovation. This pattern suggests the book serves as a reference point for researchers investigating how austerity measures intersected with educational outcomes, labor policy, and social reform during the Europe 2020 period. Notably, one citing author (Javier Ramos Jim\u00e9nez) is among the book's original authors, indicating continued scholarly development of these themes.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Library holdings and subject cataloging reveal institutional recognition across Spanish and European research environments. WorldCat records list the work under categories spanning Spanish economic and social policy, European Union commercial and social policy, and EU economic policy\u2014confirming its relevance to policy studies and comparative European governance. The book's open-access status through Peter Lang Verlag enhances accessibility, and the work is registered in OpenAIRE repositories, supporting discoverability within the European research infrastructure. The work's alignment with Sustainable Development Goal 17 (Partnerships for the Goals) reflects its engagement with multilevel governance and cross-national policy coordination challenges.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">As a monograph entering its seventh year of publication, this work represents the kind of specialized policy analysis that circulates within academic and professional networks rather than generating broad public engagement. The absence of event data and media mentions reflects the book's disciplinary positioning: it contributes to scholarly conversations on labor, social policy, and European integration rather than seeking or generating popular discourse. The citation landscape\u2014concentrated among Spanish and Northern European institutions and focused on education and social inequality themes\u2014suggests the work remains a valid reference for scholars analyzing the distributional consequences of fiscal consolidation in contemporary Europe.</p>",
